 {
  "id": "L20/p1",
  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>The Chinchilla rule of thumb is about 20 training tokens per parameter. By that rule, how many parameters should a model trained on 1 million tokens have?</p>",
  "answer": "50000",
  "answerKind": "numeric",
  "tol": 0.05,
  "options": [],
  "explain": "<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>20</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>50,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^6 / 20 = 50{,}000</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0641em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">6</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">/20</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8389em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span><span class=\"mord\">000</span></span></span></span> parameters. Yet the model of L15, with 10.7 million parameters trained for about 40 epochs with dropout, reaches a validation loss far below what a 50,000-parameter model can reach (Karpathy's nanoGPT reports about 1.47 nats per character for this configuration). The Chinchilla rule answers a different question: the best split of a fixed <strong>compute</strong> budget when data is unlimited. Here data is the limit and compute is plentiful, so the right move is a larger model that sees the same data many times, with regularization and early stopping on a validation set (Muennighoff et al., 2023, measure how repeated data loses value). Your design document has to make exactly this kind of argument.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L20/p2",
  "lesson": "L20",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>The pipeline pretrains, fine-tunes with LoRA, then runs RL against an exact-match reward. Which evaluation tells you whether RL helped?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>The RL reward at the end of training.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Accuracy on the training prompts before and after RL.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>Accuracy with confidence intervals on held-out prompts, before and after RL, compared item by item, with the base model's loss on ordinary text to show what was lost.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>The KL divergence from the reference policy.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>Training reward measures what was optimized, on the inputs it was optimized on; it can rise through memorization or reward hacking. A held-out, paired comparison with intervals (L19) tells you whether the change generalizes and whether it is larger than noise, and the base loss measures forgetting (L16). The KL is a diagnostic of how far the policy moved, not of whether it improved.</p>"
 },
